      { "text": "Single point mutation in the beta-globin gene replacing glutamic acid (hydrophilic) with valine (hydrophobic) at codon 6.", "explanation": "Correct. The lecturer specified the classic sickle cell mutation: a single point mutation (GAG → GTG) in the beta-globin gene that substitutes valine (hydrophobic) for glutamic acid (hydrophilic) at the 6th amino acid residue. This polarity change (polar → non-polar) allows deoxygenated hemoglobin S (HbS) molecules to polymerize, distorting the red cell into the sickle shape. Sickled cells become rigid, lose deformability, and obstruct microvasculature, producing vaso-occlusive crises and microangiopathic hemolysis. Inheritance is autosomal recessive; heterozygotes have sickle cell trait and are largely protected from clinical disease." }
